deep.reconstruction:
This page is not Grothendieck's own mathematics. It is a typescript sheet, photographed upside down, numbered 'n°370 - 6 -'. It comes from a Bourbaki rédaction (draft no. 370) of what became Intégration, Chapter VII, §1, no. 2, 'Le théorème d'existence et d'unicité' for Haar measure. Grothendieck evidently used the back of Bourbaki drafts as scrap paper for his 1965 notes on the Galois theory of motives. The neighbouring pages are handwritten notes on motives, Hodge structures and L-functions, and are unrelated in content.

The mathematics is the standard Haar–Weil existence proof.
- Notation: K = continuous compactly supported real functions on a locally compact group G; K_+ = the nonnegative ones; K_+^* = K_+ \ {0}; K_+^*(C) = those supported in a compact set C; gamma(s) = left translation.
- For f in K and g in K_+^*, f is dominated by a finite combination sum c_i gamma(s_i) g with c_i >= 0. Reason: g is bounded below by a positive constant on some nonempty open U, and supp f is covered by finitely many left translates of U.
- The Haar covering number (f:g) is the infimum of sum c_i over all such dominations.
- Properties listed:
  (i) left invariance;
  (ii) positive homogeneity;
  (iii) subadditivity;
  (iv) (f:g) >= sup f / sup g;
  (v) (f:h) <= (f:g)(g:h);
  (vi) the normalization bounds 1/(f_0:f) <= (f:g)/(f_0:g) <= (f:f_0);
  (vii) approximate additivity: for g supported in a small enough compact neighbourhood V of e, (f:g)+(f':g) <= (f+f':g) + eps(h:g).
- The proof then continues: the normalized functionals (f:g)/(f_0:g) have a cluster point along the filter of shrinking supports, which yields a left-invariant positive linear form. Uniqueness is stated in Theorem 1 and proved later.

The problem served is the existence and uniqueness up to scalar of left (or right) Haar measure on a locally compact group.
